WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.819
Lots of "Law &amp; Order" episode ideas are ripped
from the headlines, but one proved so painful

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:08.350
that NBC will never air it again.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.440
Which one was it?

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.120
Watch and see.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.800
When "Law &amp; Order" made its debut on NBC in
1990, the procedural crime drama offered a

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.019
novel concept for the small screen.

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:21.619
One part of the show presents detectives working
to solve a criminal case, while the other

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:26.089
half depicts prosecutors working in the courtroom
to process that same case.

00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:30.060
It's a formula that successfully launched
many spin-offs and one that fans will enjoy

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.410
yet again when "Law &amp; Order" finally runs
its 21st season after over a decade off the

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.410
air.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.660
But "Law &amp; Order" also has another significant
story element its writers use to help anchor

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.910
its aim for gritty realism.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:47.160
Something that went a long way in making this
realistic approach possible was the tendency

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.530
for "Law &amp; Order" to use actual criminal cases
from the news as the basis for some of its

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.530
episodes.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:57.930
Even some of its offshoots, like "Law &amp; Order:
SVU," have produced numerous tales inspired

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:02.610
by true crimes throughout their runs, turning
real-life tragedy into harrowing fiction.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:06.939
However, when pulling from news headlines
for story inspiration, it's incredibly important

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:10.929
to do so in a manner that's just as tasteful
as it is entertaining.

00:01:10.929 --> 00:01:14.630
After all, the real people the episodes are
based on have real trauma.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.939
“[I am not going to apologize for doing
what needs to be done to put this monster

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:19.979
behind bars.”

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:20.979
https://youtu.be/mfMcpkSLeoU?t=339 to 5:45]

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.609
As one could imagine, attempts at this approach
are prone to go awry from time to time.

00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:29.579
For example, a 2001 episode of "Law &amp; Order"
caused so much controversy that NBC never

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:30.770
aired it again.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:35.079
The "Law &amp; Order" installment that caused
quite a stir with viewers was Season 11's

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:37.920
11th episode, "Sunday in the Park with Jorge
[HOR-hay]."

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.940
The plot dealt primarily with a millionaire's
wife being discovered dead in Central Park

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.469
following the annual Puerto Rican [POHR-tuh
REE-kin] Day Parade.

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:49.350
Meanwhile, a riot broke out during the event
where several other women were horrifically

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.350
sexually assaulted.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.240
Much like many episodes of "Law &amp; Order,"
"Sunday in the Park with Jorge" was loosely

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.899
based on a real and terrible event, but what
set it apart from all of the others and prompted

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:01.079
NBC to scrub it from television?

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:04.850
When the episode premiered, not much time
had passed since the Puerto Rican Day Parade

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.999
attacks, with them occurring only a year prior.

00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:11.849
During the event, several women came forward
with their assault stories — moments that

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:15.950
surely none of them would've liked to revisit
in the form of a TV drama.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.470
According to the Chicago Tribune, NBC held
a meeting with activist groups like the National

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.590
Puerto Rican Coalition, who felt that the
episode gave an offensive depiction of members

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.250
of the Latino community.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.159
The network heard out their complaints, and
as a result, the network decided never to

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:31.599
air the episode again.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:36.060
Now 20 years beyond its debut, the banishment
of "Sunday in the Park with Jorge" from the

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.010
airwaves still stands.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.390
While this may seem like an overall good decision
that wouldn’t really invite much pushback,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.340
not everyone agreed with NBC's choice.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.310
Arguably the loudest voice in that crowd was
the mastermind behind "Law &amp; Order" himself,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.310
Dick Wolf.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.090
He made no bones about his disagreement with
the network in the days following the announcement

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.090
of its verdict.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.060
The Chicago Tribune quoted him as saying,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.790
"The network has caved in to the demands of
a special interest group, and I am extremely

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.510
disappointed with this decision."

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.640
He added that NBC's lack of communication
with him during the deliberation process was

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.299
particularly irritating.

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:14.299
He also posited that the move sets an extremely
dangerous precedent.

00:03:14.299 --> 00:03:18.629
The Washington Post quoted a statement from
Wolf asserting that the show “reflects real

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:23.120
life,” and virtually any member of any group
can fit the antagonist role given certain

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.030
narrative circumstances.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.510
The "Law &amp; Order" franchise has aired many
episodes that fans might find too hard to

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.489
watch, and for many, that's a part of the
appeal.

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:36.920
It's a dramatized yet realistic look at our
world created for entertainment.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.319
Nevertheless, some have argued that it has
occasionally gone too far for a network TV

00:03:41.319 --> 00:03:47.860
show, which is exactly why NBC sought to avoid
the trouble and never rerun the episode again.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:52.420
If you or anyone you know has been a victim
of sexual assault, help is available.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:57.969
Visit the Rape, Abuse &amp; Incest National Network
website or contact RAINN's National Helpline

00:03:57.969 --> 00:04:01.650
at 1-800-656-HOPE (4673).

